### What is a type pattern in a switch?

A type pattern tests the selector's runtime type and binds a variable, replacing the old `instanceof` and cast chain.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TypePatternDemo {

    sealed interface PaymentEvent permits TransferEvent, DepositEvent {
    }
    record TransferEvent(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}
    record DepositEvent(String account, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}

    public String describe(PaymentEvent event) {
        return switch (event) {
            case TransferEvent t -> "Transfer: " + t.amount() + " INR";
            case DepositEvent d -> "Deposit: " + d.amount() + " INR";
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TypePatternDemo demo = new TypePatternDemo();
        System.out.println(demo.describe(
                new TransferEvent("SAV-1001", "SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("50000"))));
    }
}
```

```text
Transfer: 50000 INR
```

### What is a record pattern?

A record pattern goes further and destructures the record's components into named bindings in the same case, so you immediately have the fields without calling accessors.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RecordPatternDemo {

    sealed interface PaymentEvent permits TransferEvent, DepositEvent,
            WithdrawalEvent {}
    record TransferEvent(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}
    record DepositEvent(String account, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}
    record WithdrawalEvent(String account, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}

    public String process(PaymentEvent event) {
        return switch (event) {
            case TransferEvent(var from, var to, var amount) ->
                    "Transfer " + amount + " INR: " + from + " -> " + to;
            case DepositEvent(var account, var amount) ->
                    "Deposit " + amount + " INR to " + account;
            case WithdrawalEvent(var account, var amount) ->
                    "Withdraw " + amount + " INR from " + account;
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        RecordPatternDemo demo = new RecordPatternDemo();
        System.out.println(demo.process(
                new TransferEvent("SAV-1001", "SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("75000"))));
        System.out.println(demo.process(
                new DepositEvent("SAV-1001", new BigDecimal("25000"))));
        System.out.println(demo.process(
                new WithdrawalEvent("SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("10000"))));
    }
}
```

```text
Transfer 75000 INR: SAV-1001 -> SAV-2002
Deposit 25000 INR to SAV-1001
Withdraw 10000 INR from SAV-2002
```

### How do guarded patterns work?

Guards refine a case with a boolean condition using the `when` keyword, so you branch on values, not just on shape.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class GuardedPatternDemo {

    sealed interface PaymentEvent permits TransferEvent, DepositEvent {}
    record TransferEvent(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}
    record DepositEvent(String account, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}

    private static final BigDecimal HIGH_VALUE = new BigDecimal("100000");

    public String classify(PaymentEvent event) {
        return switch (event) {
            case TransferEvent(var f, var t, var amount)
                    when amount.compareTo(HIGH_VALUE) > 0 ->
                    "HIGH VALUE transfer " + amount + " INR (needs approval)";
            case TransferEvent(var f, var t, var amount) ->
                    "Normal transfer " + amount + " INR";
            case DepositEvent(var account, var amount) ->
                    "Deposit " + amount + " INR";
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        GuardedPatternDemo demo = new GuardedPatternDemo();
        System.out.println(demo.classify(
                new TransferEvent("SAV-1001", "SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("250000"))));
        System.out.println(demo.classify(
                new TransferEvent("SAV-1001", "SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("5000"))));
    }
}
```

```text
HIGH VALUE transfer 250000 INR (needs approval)
Normal transfer 5000 INR
```

### How does exhaustiveness work with sealed hierarchies?

If you declare a sealed interface that permits exactly three types, the compiler knows the complete set. A switch with a case for each is provably exhaustive and needs no `default`. If someone adds a fourth type, every non-exhaustive switch fails to compile.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ExhaustiveDemo {

    sealed interface PaymentEvent permits TransferEvent, DepositEvent,
            WithdrawalEvent {}
    record TransferEvent(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}
    record DepositEvent(String account, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}
    record WithdrawalEvent(String account, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}

    public BigDecimal calculateFee(PaymentEvent event) {
        return switch (event) {
            case TransferEvent t -> new BigDecimal("25.00");
            case DepositEvent d -> BigDecimal.ZERO;
            case WithdrawalEvent w -> new BigDecimal("10.00");
            // No default: compiler proves this is exhaustive
        };
    }

    // If we add: record RefundEvent(...) implements PaymentEvent {}
    // This switch FAILS TO COMPILE until we handle RefundEvent
}
```

### How do nested record patterns work?

When a record holds another record, you can destructure both in a single pattern.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class NestedPatternDemo {

    record AccountInfo(String id, BigDecimal balance) {}

    sealed interface Txn permits Transfer, Deposit {}
    record Transfer(AccountInfo from, AccountInfo to, BigDecimal amount)
            implements Txn {}
    record Deposit(AccountInfo account, BigDecimal amount)
            implements Txn {}

    public String process(Txn txn) {
        return switch (txn) {
            case Transfer(AccountInfo(var fromId, var fromBal),
                          AccountInfo(var toId, var toBal), var amount) ->
                    fromId + " (" + fromBal + ") -> " + toId
                            + " (" + toBal + "): " + amount + " INR";
            case Deposit(AccountInfo(var id, var bal), var amount) ->
                    "Deposit " + amount + " INR to " + id + " (" + bal + ")";
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NestedPatternDemo demo = new NestedPatternDemo();
        System.out.println(demo.process(new Transfer(
                new AccountInfo("SAV-1001", new BigDecimal("500000")),
                new AccountInfo("SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("200000")),
                new BigDecimal("75000")
        )));
    }
}
```

```text
SAV-1001 (500000) -> SAV-2002 (200000): 75000 INR
```

### How does null handling work?

A switch with patterns throws on `null` unless you add an explicit `case null`, which removes a common source of bugs.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class NullHandlingDemo {

    sealed interface PaymentEvent permits TransferEvent {}
    record TransferEvent(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount)
            implements PaymentEvent {}

    public String process(PaymentEvent event) {
        return switch (event) {
            case null -> "No event received";
            case TransferEvent(var f, var t, var amt) -> "Transfer: " + amt;
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NullHandlingDemo demo = new NullHandlingDemo();
        System.out.println(demo.process(null));
        System.out.println(demo.process(
                new TransferEvent("SAV-1001", "SAV-2002", new BigDecimal("50000"))));
    }
}
```

```text
No event received
Transfer: 50000
```

### Why should you avoid default over sealed types?

A `default` branch would silently swallow a newly added subtype, defeating the exhaustiveness guarantee. Without `default`, adding a new permitted type forces the compiler to flag every switch that does not handle it.

```java
// WRONG: default hides new subtypes
// return switch (event) {
//     case TransferEvent t -> ...;
//     default -> "Unknown"; // silently swallows new subtypes!
// };

// CORRECT: no default, compiler-enforced exhaustiveness
return switch (event) {
    case TransferEvent t -> "Transfer";
    case DepositEvent d -> "Deposit";
    case WithdrawalEvent w -> "Withdrawal";
};
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Pattern matching, record patterns, and sealed types together form Java's answer to algebraic data types. The compiler enforces case ordering: a more general pattern cannot precede a more specific one. Guards with `when` layer business logic without breaking exhaustiveness. The switch can be an expression that yields a value, forcing every path to produce a result. In a banking system at National Bank of India, Pankaj replaced long `instanceof` chains with one exhaustive pattern-matching switch per processor, and every new event type added is caught at compile time across the entire codebase.
